WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.020
Size your label, cost your pack, then test it.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.530
This is the video for Module 2 of Product Packaging and Branding.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:16.780
Many first labels fail for simple reasons. The label is too long and overlaps badly, or the text is cut off at the edge.

4
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:22.080
Or the writing is too small to read, and the printed colours look dull.

5
00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.510
And forgotten costs, like the seal or carton share, eat your profit.

6
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:35.010
Problems found now are cheap to fix. Problems found after a large print run cost much more.

7
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:45.950
For example, Bisi in Ibadan packs groundnut butter in round jars. The body is 70 mm across and the straight part is 70 mm tall.

8
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:56.710
The distance around a round jar is the diameter times 3.14. So 70 times 3.14 is about 220 mm.

9
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.790
She adds 5 mm of overlap so the ends meet neatly. Her label is 225 mm long.

10
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.070
She leaves 5 mm clear at the top and bottom, so the label is 60 mm tall.

11
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:24.930
In Canva, she adds 3 mm of bleed on every side. Bleed is extra background beyond the cut line, so no white edge shows. Her canvas is 231 by 66 mm.

12
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:31.450
And she keeps every word at least 3 mm inside the cut line. This is the safe zone.

13
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:43.900
She exports PDF Print, prints one copy at 100%, not fit to page, and wraps it on a real jar. Screens glow, so always approve a printed sample.

14
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.420
On the front, the most important thing is biggest. First, your brand name.

15
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:51.990
Then the product name.

16
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.850
Then one short key point, such as no added sugar, but only if it is true.

17
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:06.690
And the net content, such as 250 g. The ingredients, maker's address, dates and batch number go on the back.

18
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:21.170
Now the cost. Bisi lists every packaging item: jar and lid 220 naira, label 60, foil seal 15, and a carton share of 25. That makes 320 naira.

19
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:33.430
She adds 5% for wastage, for labels that go on crooked or jars that crack. 320 times 1.05 is 336 naira.

20
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.350
The groundnut butter inside costs 750 naira.

21
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.900
Her labour is 150 naira.

22
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.880
And her share of transport, data and rent is 100 naira.

23
00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:54.920
The full cost of one jar is 1,336 naira. Packaging is about a quarter of it.

24
00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:02.230
To earn a 30% margin on the selling price, divide the full cost by 0.70.

25
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:10.130
That is about 1,909, which she rounds to 1,900 naira for shops.

26
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:23.730
To leave the shop about 25% of its selling price, she divides 1,900 by 0.75. That suggests about 2,500 naira on the shelf.

27
00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.800
Then she checks similar products in two supermarkets.

28
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:41.000
Be careful. Adding 30% to a cost of 1,000 naira gives 1,300, but the margin is only about 23%. To get a true margin, divide.

29
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:51.720
A short run of 100 labels costs about 6,000 naira, or 60 naira per label. Little cash is tied up, and it is perfect for testing.

30
00:03:52.970 --> 00:04:06.030
A run of 1,000 costs about 25,000 naira, only 25 naira each. But if your design or NAFDAC details change, unused labels become waste. Test with short runs first.

31
00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:13.010
And when buying packaging online, ask for a sample first, and pay only into an account in the company's name.

32
00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:25.290
Make five to ten real prototypes and try to break them. Turn liquid or oily products upside down on a paper towel for 24 hours. No marks means a pass.

33
00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:39.470
Keep two in a warm room for two or three days, safely away from children. Put chilled drinks in a cooler. For example, Funke's paper zobo labels wrinkled in the ice, so she switched to waterproof vinyl.

34
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.690
Send two on a real delivery, and photograph your product among competitors from two metres away.

35
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.950
Then ask five buyers what it is and what they would pay, not do you like it. Change one thing per round, and retest.

36
00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:03.710
To remember. Add overlap and bleed, keep words in the safe zone, cost every item, divide to price, and test before a big run.

37
00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:12.090
Now design version 1 of your label, cost one unit completely, and run two rounds of testing with a test log.

38
00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.380
Continue with the lessons below.
